#==============================================================================
#            Schaefer-Turek 3D cylinder benchmark, case 3D-2Z
#
#   The published unsteady case: a channel 2.5 x 0.41 x 0.41 with a cylinder of
#   diameter 0.1 spanning the full z extent, its axis at (0.5, 0.2), and a
#   parabolic inlet whose mean velocity is 1.0. With a kinematic viscosity of
#   1e-3 -- which is what re = 1000 means here, since the solver multiplies the
#   viscous term by 1/re in the domain's own units -- the Reynolds number formed
#   on the cylinder diameter is 100.
#
#   The geometry is the analytic cylinder rather than a voxel volume on purpose:
#   a rasterized body is only as accurate as its voxel size, and that would cap
#   the very quantity this benchmark measures.
#
#   The inlet prescribes the velocity, so the pressure there takes a zero normal
#   gradient (bcLeft 1) and only the outlet pins it. Labelling the inlet an
#   outflow would pin the pressure at both ends of a channel whose inflow is
#   already prescribed, which over-determines the pressure problem.
#==============================================================================

# Problem specific Data:
# ---------------------

name schaefer-turek    # selects the parabolic inflow

bcLeft    1			#  prescribed inflow, so a zero normal pressure gradient
bcRight   3			#  outflow, which pins the pressure
bcBottom  1			#  1 = no-slip      3 = outflow
bcTop     1			#  2 = free-slip    4 = periodic
bcFront   1			#
bcBack    1			#

gx     0.0      # Body forces (e.g. gravity)
gy     0.0      #
gz     0.0      #

re            1000.0   # 1 / kinematic viscosity, giving nu = 1e-3

u_init        0.0      # initial value for velocity in x-direction
v_init        0.0      # initial value for velocity in y-direction
w_init        0.0      # initial value for velocity in z-direction
p_init        0.0      # initial value for pressure

# Geometry Data:
# -------------

xlength       2.5      # domain size in x-direction
ylength       0.41	   # domain size in y-direction
zlength       0.41	   # domain size in z-direction
imax          256      # number of interior cells in x-direction
jmax          48	   # number of interior cells in y-direction
kmax          48	   # number of interior cells in z-direction

# Obstacle Geometry Data:
# -----------------------
# Diameter 0.1 at (0.5, 0.2), spanning z. About ten cells across, which is what
# the two-dimensional change found is too few for the wake to shed.

geometryFile  cylinder-z:0.5,0.2,0.05

# Time Data:
# ---------

te       8.0    # final time
dt       0.005  # time stepsize
tau      0.5    # safety factor for time stepsize control (<0 constant delt)

# Multigrid data:
# ---------

# Three levels, not four: the cylinder is ten cells across on the finest grid,
# so at the fourth level it would be under two coarse cells and would round
# away. The solver reports that rather than hiding it, but a hierarchy whose
# coarsest level cannot see the body is not worth building.
#
# Kept at three when the other setups were given the depth their grids support.
# This grid coarsens to five levels, and the cylinder stops being represented at
# level 3 of 4 -- measured, not assumed -- so every level this setup could gain
# is one whose coarse correction is blind to the body. That matters more here
# than elsewhere because this is the validation benchmark: its drag and lift are
# what it exists to produce, and they are what a correction computed without the
# cylinder would move.
#
# The coarse solve is what the extra depth would have bought, and that is now
# strong enough on its own that the depth is not needed to compensate for it.
# karman.par is left alone for a different reason, its grid; this one is left
# alone for the body.
levels        3         # Multigrid levels (should be >= 1)
presmooth     5         # Pre-smoothning iterations
postsmooth    5         # Post-smoothning iterations

# Pressure Iteration Data:
# -----------------------

itermax       50000     # maximal number of pressure iteration in one time step
eps           0.0001    # stopping tolerance for pressure iteration
omg           1.8       # relaxation parameter for SOR iteration
gamma         0.9       # upwind differencing factor gamma
#===============================================================================
